  "content": {
    "id": "p27-q4-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "4",
    "prompt": "Determine whether each function is a PDF. If it is, find its mode.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p27-q4-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "$f(x)=3x^2$, where $0\\le x\\le1$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "PDF; mode $=1$.",
          "worked": "$f(x)\\ge0$ on $[0,1]$.\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\int_0^1 3x^2\\,dx&=[x^3]_0^1\\\\&=1\\end{aligned}$$\n\nThus $f$ is a PDF. Since $f'(x)=6x>0$ for $0<x<1$, $f$ is increasing on $[0,1]$. Its maximum is at the right endpoint, so the mode is $1$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 25
      },
      {
        "id": "p27-q4-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "$f(x)=\\frac14x$, where $1\\le x\\le5$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Not a PDF.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{aligned}\\int_1^5\\frac14x\\,dx&=\\left[\\frac{x^2}{8}\\right]_1^5\\\\&=\\frac{25-1}{8}\\\\&=3\\ne1\\end{aligned}$$\n\nThe total area is not $1$, so $f$ is not a PDF.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 18
      },
      {
        "id": "p27-q4-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "$f(x)=\\frac{4-2x}{3}$, where $0\\le x\\le3$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Not a PDF.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{aligned}\\int_0^3\\frac{4-2x}{3}\\,dx&=\\left[\\frac{4x}{3}-\\frac{x^2}{3}\\right]_0^3\\\\&=4-3\\\\&=1\\end{aligned}$$\n\nHowever, $f(x)<0$ for $2<x\\le3$. A PDF must be nonnegative throughout its domain, so $f$ is not a PDF.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 23
      },
      {
        "id": "p27-q4-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "$f(x)=(n+1)x^n$, where $0\\le x\\le1$, $n>0$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "PDF; mode $=1$.",
          "worked": "Since $n>0$, $f(x)\\ge0$ on $[0,1]$.\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\int_0^1(n+1)x^n\\,dx&=[x^{n+1}]_0^1\\\\&=1\\end{aligned}$$\n\nThus $f$ is a PDF. For $0<x<1$, $f'(x)=n(n+1)x^{n-1}>0$, so $f$ is increasing on $[0,1]$. Its maximum is at $x=1$, so the mode is $1$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 26
      },
      {
        "id": "p27-q4-e",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": "$f(x)=\\frac12\\sin x$, where $0\\le x\\le\\pi$",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "PDF; mode $=\\frac\\pi2$.",
          "worked": "$f(x)\\ge0$ on $[0,\\pi]$.\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\int_0^\\pi\\frac12\\sin x\\,dx&=\\frac12[-\\cos x]_0^\\pi\\\\&=\\frac12(1+1)\\\\&=1\\end{aligned}$$\n\nThus $f$ is a PDF. On this interval, sine reaches its maximum value of $1$ at $x=\\frac\\pi2$. Therefore the mode is $\\frac\\pi2$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 26
      }
    ]
  },
